  "how": "**Install:** run this from the tool's folder, then type `/reload-skills` in any open session (or start a new one).\n\n```powershell\npython -I tools/install_kit.py --to \"$HOME\\.claude\" crisp\n```\n\nOr download SKILL.md below and save it as `%USERPROFILE%\\.claude\\skills\\crisp\\SKILL.md`.\n\n**Use:** type `/crisp`, then the question and the facts you know:\n\n```text\n/crisp Manager asks: \"is saved search on track for Friday?\" Backend done, UI 3 of 5 screens, QA env down until Monday.\n```\n\nClaude can also pick it up on its own when you paste a colleague's question and ask how to reply. For a message that asks named people for a decision, your `stakeholder-brief` skill is still the right one.",
  "files": [
   {
    "path": "skills/crisp/SKILL.md",
    "install": "~/.claude/skills/crisp/SKILL.md",
    "text": "---\nname: crisp\ndescription: Write a short, plain reply to a quick question from a manager or teammate (status, \"when will it be done?\", yes or no, \"why did it break?\", \"can we add X?\"), ready to paste into Slack or Teams. Use when the user says crisp, short answer, for my manager, what do I tell them, or pastes a colleague's question and asks how to reply. Not for a message asking named people for a decision (use stakeholder-brief) and not for investigations.\nargument-hint: \"[the question and the facts you know]\"\n---\n\n# Crisp reply\n\nThe user will paste your reply into a chat with their manager or a colleague. Write it the way a\nsenior engineer answers a colleague: the answer first, in plain words, and nothing else.\n\nInvoking this skill is an explicit request for brevity. It overrides any standing instruction to\nexplain in several labelled parts or to pre-answer follow-up questions; those are for\ninvestigations, not for quick replies.\n\n## Input\n\n$ARGUMENTS\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Work out the real question: status, when, yes or no, why, or \"can we add\".\n2. Use only facts the user gave, or facts you checked in this session (git log, ROADMAP.md, test\n   output). If the answer needs a fact you do not have, most often a date, ask for it in one line\n   instead of inventing it.\n3. Write the reply by the rules below, then check it against them before you show it.\n\n## Rules for the reply\n\n- The first words answer the question: \"Yes\", \"No\", \"Not for Friday\", \"Tue 27 Oct\".\n- At most 3 sentences and 60 words. No sentence over 25 words.\n- Plain words. No greeting, no preamble, no sign-off, no headings, no bullet points, no emoji, at\n  most one dash.\n- A \"when\" question gets a date or a range, how sure you are, and what it depends on.\n- Bad news is said plainly, with the new date and what you need. No apology.\n- Never use: Great question, Certainly, I'd be happy to, I hope this helps, Let me know if you have\n  any questions, It's worth noting, delve, deep dive, robust, seamless, leverage, comprehensive,\n  Overall, In summary, Furthermore, Additionally, You're absolutely right, Essentially, it seems,\n  potentially.\n\n## Output\n\nExactly this, and nothing before it:\n\n```\n━━━ COPY FROM HERE ━━━\n<the reply>\n━━━ COPY TO HERE ━━━\n```\n\nIf they ask more:\n- <up to 3 short facts to keep ready; leave this part out when there is nothing useful>\n\nLast, only if a fact in the reply is unverified, one line: \"Check before sending: <the fact>\".\n"
   }
  ]
