    "install": "~/.claude/skills/phase-plan/SKILL.md",
    "text": "---\nname: phase-plan\ndescription: Turn a PRD, ticket or feature description into a phased plan in ROADMAP.md, where each phase leaves something usable, fits in one session, and has a \"done means\" that a command or test can prove. Use when starting a big feature, or when the user asks for phased development, \"phase 1, phase 2\", or wants to build across several sessions with /clear between them.\nargument-hint: \"[path to the PRD or a short description]\"\n---\n\n# Plan a feature in phases\n\nThe user builds big features one phase per Claude Code session and runs /clear between phases to\nkeep each session short and cheap. Everything the next session needs must therefore be in files:\nROADMAP.md holds the plan and, later, what each phase delivered. Do not write code in this skill.\n\n## Input\n\n$ARGUMENTS\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read the requirement (the PRD path or the description above), the repo's README and CLAUDE.md,\n   and the existing feature most similar to this one. For a large repo, or several repos, use one\n   Explore subagent per repo so the reads stay out of this conversation.\n2. List the requirements as numbered, testable statements, and the open questions with a proposed\n   default for each. If an open question would change the plan, show the list and wait for the\n   user's answers before planning.\n3. Plan 3 to 6 phases (more only when the feature really is bigger). Every phase must:\n   - leave something usable and testable: no phase that is only setup or research; phase 1 is a\n     thin slice that works end to end;\n   - fit in one session, about a day of work;\n   - have a \"done means\" line that a test, a command or a screenshot can prove;\n   - for work across repos: change shared contracts first (backwards compatible), then the side\n     that provides them, then the side that uses them.\n4. Write ROADMAP.md in the shape below. If ROADMAP.md already exists, show what you would change\n   and ask before replacing anything.\n5. Print the exact prompt to start phase 1 in a fresh session, and tell the user: approve, run\n   /clear, then paste it (or type /phase-next).\n\n## ROADMAP.md shape\n\n```markdown\n# Roadmap: <feature>\n\n<one or two lines: the goal, and the PRD or ticket link>\n\nEach phase runs in a fresh Claude Code session (/clear first), so every session stays short. To\nstart a phase, say: \"Build phase N of <feature>; read ROADMAP.md first.\" or type /phase-next.\n\n| Phase | What it delivers | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 <name> | <what a user or a test can see> | planned |\n\n## Phase 1: <name>\n\n**Delivers:** <...>\n\n**Done means:** <checkable: tests, commands, screenshots>\n\n## Decisions and open questions\n\n- <decision or question, with who decides>\n\n## How every phase is done\n\n1. Read this file; restate the phase's \"done means\"; plan it and wait for approval.\n2. Write the tests first and watch them fail.\n3. Build until they pass; run <the project's test command>.\n4. Update this file: mark the phase done and add \"What phase N delivered\" (built, changed from the\n   plan, not checked).\n5. Commit following the repo's conventions, then /clear before the next phase.\n```\n\nFollow the repo's own process where it has one (CLAUDE.md, CONTRIBUTING.md, a PR template): its\nbranch names, commit format and test rules go into \"How every phase is done\".\n"
