    "text": "---\nname: phase-next\ndescription: Start the next phase of a feature planned in ROADMAP.md, in a fresh session - read the roadmap, find the first phase not done, restate its \"done means\", plan it and wait for approval, then build it tests first. Use after /clear when the user says next phase, continue the feature, or build phase N.\nargument-hint: \"[phase number (optional)]\"\n---\n\n# Start the next phase\n\nThis session started fresh (after /clear), so you know nothing about the feature yet. The files\nknow everything: rebuild the context from them and nothing else.\n\n## Input\n\nPhase number, if given: $ARGUMENTS\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read ROADMAP.md, then README.md and HANDOFF.md if they exist. Use the phase number above if one\n   was given; otherwise the first phase not marked done. If HANDOFF.md describes this phase, carry\n   on from its \"next step\".\n2. Tell the user in at most 3 lines: which phase, its \"done means\", and what the earlier phases\n   delivered that this one builds on.\n3. Plan the phase: the files to change and the tests to write. Present the plan and wait for the\n   user's approval before editing anything.\n4. After approval, write the tests from the \"done means\" first. Run them and show them failing for\n   the right reason. Then build until they pass, and run the project's full test command from\n   ROADMAP.md. Show the real output.\n5. Stay inside this phase. If it turns out bigger than one session, stop and propose how to split\n   it, updating ROADMAP.md only after the user agrees.\n6. When every part of the \"done means\" is proven with real output, say so and suggest /phase-close.\n   If context gets above about 60% before that, offer to write HANDOFF.md so the user can /clear.\n"
