    "text": "---\nname: phase-close\ndescription: Close the current phase of a feature - prove its \"done means\" with real test output, record in ROADMAP.md what the phase delivered, what changed from the plan and what was not checked, commit, and print the exact prompt for the next phase so the user can /clear.\nargument-hint: \"[phase number (optional)]\"\ndisable-model-invocation: true\n---\n\n# Close the phase\n\nEverything that is not written down is lost at /clear. This skill makes sure the next session\nstarts from facts. It commits, so it only runs when the user types /phase-close.\n\n## Input\n\nPhase number, if given: $ARGUMENTS\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Find the phase: the number above, or the phase in ROADMAP.md that is in progress.\n2. Run the project's test command (from ROADMAP.md or CLAUDE.md) and show the real summary lines.\n   For each part of the phase's \"done means\", name the test or the output that proves it. If any\n   part is not proven, stop: say what is missing and do not mark the phase done.\n3. Update ROADMAP.md:\n   - set the phase's status to \"done (<today's date>)\";\n   - add \"### What phase N delivered\" under the phase, with three short lists: Built, Changed from\n     the plan, Not checked.\n4. If HANDOFF.md described this phase, delete it.\n5. Show `git status`. Commit only the files that belong to this phase, following the repo's commit\n   convention from CLAUDE.md (default: \"Phase N: <name>\"). Flag anything that looks like a secret, a\n   log or build output instead of committing it. Do not push unless the user asks.\n6. Print the prompt for the next phase in a block the user can copy, then tell them:\n   `/rename <feature>-phase-N`, `/clear`, then paste the prompt (or type /phase-next).\n   If this was the last phase, say so, and suggest opening the pull request.\n"
