    "text": "---\nname: repo-onboard\ndescription: Make a one-page map of one or more unfamiliar repositories, aimed at shipping a feature in them - what each does, stack and versions, exact build, test, lint and run commands, folder map, entry points, how the repos talk to each other, conventions, and where the most similar feature lives. Use when starting work in a repo you do not know, or on a feature that spans several repos. For exhaustive architecture documentation with diagrams, use repo-architecture instead.\nargument-hint: \"[repo paths (default: this folder and added folders)]\"\n---\n\n# Onboard to repos, fast\n\nThe goal is to be productive in these repos today, not to document them. Facts only: every\ncommand and path must come from a file you read. Mark anything you infer as \"(guess)\".\n\n## Input\n\nRepos: $ARGUMENTS\nIf none are given, use the working directory and every folder added with --add-dir.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Run one Explore subagent per repo, in parallel, each with this brief:\n   \"Map <repo> for an engineer who must ship a feature in it this week. Return at most one page:\n   purpose (2 lines); stack and versions (from package.json, Cargo.toml, tsconfig, toolchain\n   files); exact commands to install, build, test, lint and run locally (from scripts, Makefile,\n   CI workflow, README); top-level folders and what each holds; entry points (servers, CLIs,\n   handlers, pages); how it talks to other services (HTTP clients, routes, events, shared types,\n   database tables) with file:line; 5 conventions shown by the code and config (errors, logging,\n   tests, naming, layout); where tests live and how one is named. Verified facts only; mark\n   guesses.\"\n2. Show each repo's page.\n3. Add the picture across repos: every contract between them (endpoints, request and response\n   shapes, events, shared types, tables), with the file:line on each side.\n4. If the user named a feature, find the existing feature most similar to it and trace it end to\n   end, with file:line for each step. This is the template the new code should copy.\n5. For any repo without a CLAUDE.md, offer a draft of under 60 lines built from the verified facts:\n   commands first, then the folder map, then the rules. Write it only if the user agrees (as\n   CLAUDE.local.md when they cannot commit to that repo).\n"
